{# v3 structured agent-prompt macros.
   Merges three vendor patterns:
   - Anthropic 4-field subagent contract (objective / output format / tool guidance / boundaries) +
     semantic XML wrappers + tool-description-as-first-lever + altitude calibration.
   - OpenAI GPT-5 schema (Role / Personality / Goal / Success / Constraints / Output / Stop) +
     tool preambles + explicit instruction hierarchy.
   - Google ADK split: `routing_description` (peers see this) vs `instruction` (this agent obeys this) +
     `output_key`-style state hand-off naming + named multi-agent patterns.

   Structure choice: XML wrappers for top-level compartments (Anthropic / GPT-5 style),
   Markdown inside each wrapper for readability (Google ADK style). This matches the
   GPT-5.1 cookbook hybrid.

   This is a PARALLEL macro set. v2 `agent_definition.j2` is untouched. Agents migrate
   one at a time by switching their .j2 template from `agent_prompt(...)` to
   `agent_prompt_v3(...)`. Side-by-side rollout is the goal.
#}

{% from "agents/shared/agent_definition.j2" import tool_usage_rules_global %}


{# ----- internal helpers (underscore prefix = not for direct call) ----- #}

{% macro _peers_table(peers) -%}
{%- if peers %}
| Peer | Their role | When they call you | When you call them | Escalate via |
|------|------------|--------------------|---------------------|--------------|
{%- for p in peers %}
| `{{ p.id }}` | {{ p.role }} | {{ p.calls_you }} | {{ p.you_call }} | {{ p.escalate_via | default('—') }} |
{%- endfor %}
{%- else %}
_No peers — operate standalone._
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro %}


{% macro _hubs_table(hubs) -%}
{%- if hubs %}
| Hub | When you READ | When you WRITE | Tools |
|-----|---------------|----------------|-------|
{%- for h in hubs %}
| **{{ h.name }}** | {{ h.read_when }} | {{ h.write_when }} | `{{ h.tools }}` |
{%- endfor %}

The table above is your authoritative contract with each hub — follow the "When you READ" / "When you WRITE" columns. Reads should be MINIMAL and on-demand (do not enumerate or poll).
{%- else %}
_No hub interaction — this agent does not touch RegistryHub/WorkHub/CodeHub/EventHub._
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro %}


{# PR5 — tool_policy template. Final shape after migration: each entry
   is ``{tool, when, cap?}`` where ``when`` is a single positive
   condition phrase ("when X happens, call this; if X doesn't,
   escalate instead of re-checking"). No more separate ``avoid:``
   slot — the user's "我不太希望prompt里面出现DO not use之类的东西"
   guidance.

   Legacy ``{use, avoid}`` shape is intentionally NOT supported here.
   If a future edit reintroduces ``it.use`` or ``it.avoid``, the
   rendered output will be missing the policy line (Jinja2's undefined
   resolves to empty), and the closed-by-construction tests in
   tests/test_tool_policy_when_shape_invariant.py will fail —
   surfacing the regression before merge.
#}
{% macro _tool_policy(items) -%}
{%- for it in items %}
- `{{ it.tool }}` — **When**: {{ it.when }}.{% if it.cap %} **Cap**: {{ it.cap }}.{% endif %}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endmacro %}


{% macro _examples(examples) -%}
<examples>
{%- for ex in examples %}
<example>
<scenario>{{ ex.scenario }}</scenario>
{%- if ex.thinking %}
<thinking>
{{ ex.thinking }}
</thinking>
{%- endif %}
{%- if ex.actions %}
<actions>
{%- for a in ex.actions %}
- {{ a }}
{%- endfor %}
</actions>
{%- endif %}
{%- if ex.output %}
<output>
{{ ex.output }}
</output>
{%- endif %}
</example>
{%- endfor %}
</examples>
{%- endmacro %}


{# ----- the canonical v3 macro -----

   Required:
     id, name, routing_description, mandate
   Highly recommended:
     peers, hubs, success_criteria, output_contract, stop_rules
   Optional (with sensible defaults):
     step_contract_overrides   dict: stage -> one-line override
     tool_policy               list of {tool, use, avoid?, cap?}
     delegation_rubric         prose; if empty, no <delegation_rubric> block
     examples                  list of {scenario, thinking?, actions?, output?}
     include_global_tool_rules bool; default false (file-edit / team-mode rules are huge —
                               only include for agents that actually write files)
     constraints               list of one-line constraints (rendered as <constraints>)
     wake_contract             prose; for observer/event-driven agents like Knowledge.
                               If set, replaces the step_contract block since these
                               agents do not poll.
     body_extra                raw XML/Markdown string injected near the end of the
                               prompt (just before global tool rules). Use for
                               agent-specific blocks that don't fit the generic
                               fields — e.g. <knowledge_quality_rubric>, <api_response_format>.
     workspace                 default "./"
#}

